**What all the bands share.** Every band is the same physical thing — transverse, mutually perpendicular electric and magnetic fields oscillating in phase. So all of them

- travel through vacuum at the same speed $c = 3\times10^8\,\text{m s}^{-1}$;
- need no material medium;
- carry energy and momentum and are not deflected by electric or magnetic fields;
- show reflection, refraction, interference, diffraction and polarisation.

**What changes from band to band** is the frequency and wavelength, and therefore the way the waves are produced, the way they interact with matter, the instruments that detect them and the jobs they are useful for.

**The bands overlap.** The boundaries are not sharp physical walls; they are conventions, and different books quote slightly different limits. In particular, X-rays and gamma rays overlap in wavelength. They are named by **origin**: X-rays come from fast electrons (or inner-shell electron transitions), gamma rays from atomic nuclei. An X-ray and a gamma ray of the same wavelength are physically identical.
